				"Overview",
				"Varanasi is one of the world's oldest continually inhabited cities. Kashi, its ancient name, was associated with a kingdom of the same name in the first millennium BCE. The Lion capital of Ashoka at nearby Sarnath has been interpreted to be a commemoration of the Buddha's first sermon there in the fifth century BCE. In the 8th century, Adi Shankara established the worship of Shiva as an official sect of Varanasi. In the mid-second millennium several figures of the Bhakti movement were born here. They included [[Kabir]], Ravidas [[Tulsidas|and Tulsidas]]. Kabir came to influence traditions in Hinduism, Sikhism, and Sufism. Tulsidas wrote his [[Awadhi language]] epic, the Ramcharitmanas, which is a popular vernacular reworking of the Sanskrit [[Ramayana]]. In the 16th century, Rajput nobles in the service of the [[Mughal Empire|Mughal emperor]] [[Akbar]], sponsored work on Hindu temples in the city.",
